{"duration_api_ms":172806,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"f1fbeda9-4a13-48d9-959d-aaeb25604b9d","total_cost_usd":0.2563616,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10245,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"output_tokens":21447,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":13263},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10245,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":21447,"cache_read_input_tokens":4538,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10245},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":21447,"cacheReadInputTokens":4538,"cacheCreationInputTokens":10245,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.2563616,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":13263,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"merged_document\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\\n\\nEvery credential has a cap. When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge — without waking the service behind it — so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal. An earlier draft of this note argued for a client-side token bucket that mirrored the gateway's own counters, and that section has been left out on purpose — two counters that disagree are worse than one counter that refuses, which is the whole reason the header exists.\\n\\nRequests are counted against the credential that presented them, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose, and never against a connection. The count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts. Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing. A client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been — and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\\n\\nThe default allowance is 100 requests in a window, which is enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window; a caller that needs more than the default allowance while using an interactive credential is almost always doing batch work under the wrong tier. A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it, and it is not a bug in the gateway either. It is the platform declining to spend capacity that has already been promised to somebody else, and waiting is the only correct answer. Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\\n\\n## How to read a refusal\\n\\nThe refusal carries a Retry-After header, present on every refusal the gateway sends. Its value is a whole number of seconds to wait. Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract — a client that does it needs nothing else from this document. The gateway keeps no memory of who backed off politely, so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller no credit at all.\\n\\nThe body of the refusal is JSON, and it names what the gateway calls the “window” it counted in. It names the cap and the tier as well. None of those fields is a substitute for the header, and a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice. The body is there so that a human reading a log afterwards can see why the request was refused — nothing in it is meant for the retry loop.\\n\\n## How to retry well\\n\\nFour rules, given in the order they should be applied.\\n\\n1. Honour the header, every time.\\n   The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of that refusal is an entirely different one. A wait derived on the client side is a guess about a counter it cannot see. There is no case at all where a guess is the better of the two.\\n\\n2. Retry only what is safe to retry, which is a smaller set than the set of responses that aren't a success.\\n   A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path. The four cases are not interchangeable. A client that folds every non-success into one branch will retry hardest during exactly the incident the cap was installed to survive. Avoid writing that loop.\\n\\n3. Batch is different.\\n   A batch credential is allowed fifty times the default allowance, and it is measured over exactly the same window. The tier is set on the credential when it is issued and cannot be asked for per call. The window, the header and the body are identical to the interactive case, so a client written for one tier needs no change at all to run against the other. Nothing else about the two tiers differs in any way.\\n\\n4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week.\\n   A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf. The log line should carry the credential, the window and the wait; the counts are the whole of the argument, and without them a request is only a preference.\\n\\n## Other refusals and their codes\\n\\nNot all are caps. The gateway refuses a request for three reasons and only one of them is the one above. Two of the three have nothing to do with how much traffic a caller has sent in the window it is currently in. A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit and be refused before it has even been read. None of those three clears itself by waiting for a stated number of seconds.\\n\\nThe distinction matters more to a retry loop than it does to a reader. A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service, and burns that budget entirely on its own account. The log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals and no successes — which reads like an outage and isn't one. Reading the status code rather than the class it belongs to is what separates the two cases, and it costs one comparison.\\n\\n## Asking for an increase\\n\\nA cap can be raised, but not on the strength of an assertion that the current one is too small — the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is 0. Bring a week of refusal counts, the shape of the traffic across the day, and the deadline that traffic is serving. The platform team wants to know whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak. A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the cheapest fix available to anybody and the outcome everyone prefers.\\n\\nRequests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days; chasing it doesn't make it take fewer. There is no expedited path and no exception list.\",\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"title\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"# What a client should do when the gateway says 429\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base document's title kept unchanged.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"heading: retry-rules section\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"## How to retry well\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"## What the client does\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"## How to retry well\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base heading kept for the consolidated section.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"slot\": \"heading: non-cap refusals section\",\n      \"candidates\": [\n        {\"text\": \"## Other refusals and their codes\", \"document\": \"source_a.md\"},\n        {\"text\": \"## Other kinds of refusal\", \"document\": \"source_b.md\"}\n      ],\n      \"chosen\": \"## Other refusals and their codes\",\n      \"reason\": \"Base heading kept for the consolidated section.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dispositions\": [\n    {\"segment\": \"b1\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"# Rate limiting and the 429 contract for gateway clients\", \"reason\": \"Title decision kept base title.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b2\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Every credential has a cap.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b3\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"When that cap is spent the gateway answers 429 at the edge — without waking the service behind it — so the refusal costs the platform almost nothing and costs a caller that files it under transport error a great deal.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b4\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"An earlier draft of this note argued for a client-side token bucket that mirrored the gateway's own counters, and that section has been left out on purpose — two counters that disagree are worse than one counter that refuses, which is the whole reason the header exists.\", \"reason\": \"Unique fact from b, kept near-verbatim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a4\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose, and never against a connection.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b5's undisclosed-start detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b5\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Requests are counted against the credential that presented them, over a fixed window of 60 seconds whose start the gateway doesn't disclose, and never against a connection.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a4's counting rule.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b7\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The count follows the credential wherever the caller puts it, including across hosts.\", \"reason\": \"Unique cross-host detail kept, lightly reworded.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b6\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Opening a second socket therefore buys a caller nothing.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b8\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"A client spread across eight worker processes is throttled at exactly the point one process would have been — and pays for the extra file descriptors as well.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a7\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window, which is enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window; a caller that needs more than the default allowance while using an interactive credential is almost always doing batch work under the wrong tier.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b9's interactive-use and misuse detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b9\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The default allowance is 100 requests in a window, which is enough for every interactive use of this API the platform team has seen, and a credential marked for batch work is allowed 1000 in the same window; a caller that needs more than the default allowance while using an interactive credential is almost always doing batch work under the wrong tier.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a7's allowance numbers.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a8\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it, and it is not a bug in the gateway either.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b10's not-a-bug clause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b10\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A refusal is not an outage, whatever the error class in a client library happens to call it, and it is not a bug in the gateway either.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a8's error-class clause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b11\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Callers that retry at once are the largest single reason the cap is there at all.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b12\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"## How to read a refusal\", \"reason\": \"Identical heading already carried from base.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a12\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a Retry-After header, present on every refusal the gateway sends.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b14's present-on-every-refusal detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b13\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a Retry-After header, present on every refusal the gateway sends.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact as a12, folded into combined sentence.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b14\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The refusal carries a Retry-After header, present on every refusal the gateway sends.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a12's header-name statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b15\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Waiting that long and then continuing is the whole of the contract — a client that does it needs nothing else from this document.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept; editorial aside dropped.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b16\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"The gateway keeps no memory of who backed off politely, so waiting longer than the header asks earns a caller no credit at all.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b17\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"The body of the refusal is JSON, and it names what the gateway calls the “window” it counted in. It names the cap and the tier as well.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b18\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"None of those fields is a substitute for the header, and a caller that parses the body to compute its own wait is doing the same arithmetic twice.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b19\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"The body is there so that a human reading a log afterwards can see why the request was refused — nothing in it is meant for the retry loop.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b20\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"## How to retry well\", \"reason\": \"Heading decision kept base heading.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b21\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Four rules, given in the order they should be applied.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b22\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"1. Honour the header, every time.\", \"reason\": \"Extra clause duplicates content carried under rule 1's detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a23\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of that refusal is an entirely different one.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b24's differing-cause detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b23\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of that refusal is an entirely different one.\", \"reason\": \"Restates a23's arithmetic claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b24\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The gateway has already done that arithmetic, with information the caller does not have, and it does the same on the 503 path, even though the cause of that refusal is an entirely different one.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a23's arithmetic claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b25\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"A wait derived on the client side is a guess about a counter it cannot see.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b26\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"There is no case at all where a guess is the better of the two.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a26\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"2. Retry only what is safe to retry, which is a smaller set than the set of responses that aren't a success.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b27's clarifying clause.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b27\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"2. Retry only what is safe to retry, which is a smaller set than the set of responses that aren't a success.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a26's rule statement.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b28\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"A 200 wants nothing, a 429 wants the stated wait, a 500 may be retried once that wait has passed, and a 503 is the overload path.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b29\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"A client that folds every non-success into one branch will retry hardest during exactly the incident the cap was installed to survive.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b30\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Avoid writing that loop.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b31\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"A batch credential is allowed fifty times the default allowance, and it is measured over exactly the same window.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b32\", \"disposition\": \"reworded\", \"replacement\": \"The window, the header and the body are identical to the interactive case, so a client written for one tier needs no change at all to run against the other.\", \"reason\": \"Unique cross-tier detail kept, lightly reworded.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b33\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Nothing else about the two tiers differs in any way.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a35\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b34's retention period detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b34\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"4. Log every refusal seen, and keep the log for at least a week.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a35's logging rule.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b35\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"A caller that cannot say how often it was refused last week cannot argue for a larger cap, and nobody at the far end will assemble that argument on its behalf.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a37\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The log line should carry the credential, the window and the wait; the counts are the whole of the argument, and without them a request is only a preference.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b36's argument-value detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b36\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The log line should carry the credential, the window and the wait; the counts are the whole of the argument, and without them a request is only a preference.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a37's log-content detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b37\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"## Other refusals and their codes\", \"reason\": \"Heading decision kept base heading.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b38\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"The gateway refuses a request for three reasons and only one of them is the one above.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a41\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit and be refused before it has even been read.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b40's refused-before-read detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b40\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A credential may be suspended, a route may be closed for maintenance, or a body may exceed the 5 megabyte limit and be refused before it has even been read.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a41's list of three reasons.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a43\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The distinction matters more to a retry loop than it does to a reader.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b41's reader-comparison detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b41\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The distinction matters more to a retry loop than it does to a reader.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a43's retry-loop claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a44\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service, and burns that budget entirely on its own account. The log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals and no successes — which reads like an outage and isn't one.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b42/b43's outage-resemblance detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b42\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service, and burns that budget entirely on its own account. The log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals and no successes — which reads like an outage and isn't one.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a44's suspended-credential detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b43\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A loop that waits and retries against a suspended credential spends its whole budget without ever reaching the service, and burns that budget entirely on its own account. The log afterwards shows nothing at all except a long run of refusals and no successes — which reads like an outage and isn't one.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a44's suspended-credential detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a47\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A cap can be raised, but not on the strength of an assertion that the current one is too small — the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is 0.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b46's no-assertion-alone detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b46\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A cap can be raised, but not on the strength of an assertion that the current one is too small — the number of caps raised without a measurement behind them is 0.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a47's zero-measurement statistic.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b47\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"Bring a week of refusal counts, the shape of the traffic across the day, and the deadline that traffic is serving.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a49\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The platform team wants to know whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b48's before-the-number detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b48\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"The platform team wants to know whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a49's smooth-vs-bursty claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b49\", \"disposition\": \"duplicate\", \"replacement\": \"The platform team wants to know whether the load is smooth or bursty before it looks at the number at all — a burst is cheaper to smooth than it is to serve at its peak.\", \"reason\": \"Restates the burst-cheaper-to-smooth fact.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a50\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the cheapest fix available to anybody and the outcome everyone prefers.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b44/b45's cheapest-fix detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b44\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the cheapest fix available to anybody and the outcome everyone prefers.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a50's quieter-hour claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b45\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"A caller that can move half its work to a quieter hour usually gets what it needs without any change to the cap at all, which is the cheapest fix available to anybody and the outcome everyone prefers.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a50's quieter-hour claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"a51\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Requests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days; chasing it doesn't make it take fewer.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with b50/b52's chasing-doesn't-help detail.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b50\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Requests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days; chasing it doesn't make it take fewer.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a51's channel/timing claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b52\", \"disposition\": \"reconciled\", \"replacement\": \"Requests reach the platform team through the usual channel, and they are answered within two working days; chasing it doesn't make it take fewer.\", \"reason\": \"Combined with a51's channel/timing claim.\"},\n    {\"segment\": \"b51\", \"disposition\": \"superseded\", \"replacement\": \"There is no expedited path and no exception list.\", \"reason\": \"Same fact, base wording kept.\"}\n  ],\n  \"mismatch\": \"\"\n}\n```","ttft_ms":122986,"type":"result","duration_ms":172871,"uuid":"1814798f-c80b-4ed8-9b9e-3e32faac69f4","ttft_stream_ms":2053,"time_to_request_ms":63,"first_content_frame_ms":2054,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
